1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,660
In an ammonia factory, nitrogen and
hydrogen never turn fully into ammonia.

2
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:07,044
Some always stay.

3
00:00:07,444 --> 00:00:11,931
In your blood, haemoglobin grabs
oxygen in the lungs and lets it go in

4
00:00:11,931 --> 00:00:12,950
your muscles.

5
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:13,850
Both ways.

6
00:00:14,270 --> 00:00:18,767
Aarav reads about the ammonia factory
at his study table, late at night.

7
00:00:19,117 --> 00:00:22,269
If a reaction looks finished
halfway, has it stopped?

8
00:00:22,269 --> 00:00:23,917
Or is it secretly running?

9
00:00:24,417 --> 00:00:28,667
By the end, you will see the proof,
with heavy hydrogen, that it never

10
00:00:28,667 --> 00:00:29,567
stops.

11
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:32,232
Think of walking down an up escalator.

12
00:00:32,582 --> 00:00:34,006
The steps carry you up.

13
00:00:34,006 --> 00:00:35,907
You walk down at the same speed.

14
00:00:36,307 --> 00:00:39,473
You stay in one place,
but nothing has stopped.

15
00:00:39,873 --> 00:00:42,375
A reaction at equilibrium is like that.

16
00:00:42,375 --> 00:00:45,125
Forward and backward
run at the same speed.

17
00:00:45,725 --> 00:00:50,312
Let's put hydrogen and iodine on our
molecule bench, in a closed flask,

18
00:00:50,312 --> 00:00:51,355
and heat it.

19
00:00:51,705 --> 00:00:55,456
Hydrogen and iodine collide
and make hydrogen iodide.

20
00:00:55,456 --> 00:00:57,676
This is the forward reaction.

21
00:00:58,076 --> 00:00:59,478
At first, it is fast.

22
00:00:59,478 --> 00:01:02,061
There are lots of reactant
molecules to collide.

23
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:07,785
As hydrogen iodide builds up, it also
breaks back into hydrogen and iodine.

24
00:01:07,785 --> 00:01:10,021
This is the backward reaction.

25
00:01:10,421 --> 00:01:12,508
The forward reaction slows down.

26
00:01:12,508 --> 00:01:14,594
The backward reaction speeds up.

27
00:01:14,994 --> 00:01:17,384
At last, the two speeds are equal.

28
00:01:17,384 --> 00:01:19,117
The amounts stop changing.

29
00:01:19,117 --> 00:01:20,994
This is chemical equilibrium.

30
00:01:21,594 --> 00:01:23,924
We write it with a double arrow.

31
00:01:23,924 --> 00:01:27,054
H two plus I two, double arrow, two HI.

32
00:01:27,404 --> 00:01:31,005
A reaction that can run both
ways is called reversible.

33
00:01:31,405 --> 00:01:34,050
Rate means how fast a reaction goes.

34
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:38,720
Concentration means how crowded a
substance is, in moles per litre.

35
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,864
At equilibrium, the forward
rate equals the backward rate.

36
00:01:42,864 --> 00:01:45,283
So every concentration stays constant.

37
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:48,104
Now watch the graph, step by step.

38
00:01:48,454 --> 00:01:49,354
Step one.

39
00:01:49,175 --> 00:01:52,972
The forward rate starts high and
falls, as reactants get used up.

40
00:01:53,722 --> 00:01:54,622
Step two.

41
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:58,726
The backward rate starts at zero
and rises, as product builds up.

42
00:01:59,476 --> 00:02:00,376
Step three.

43
00:02:00,268 --> 00:02:01,551
The two curves meet.

44
00:02:01,551 --> 00:02:03,765
From here on, both rates stay equal.

45
00:02:04,515 --> 00:02:05,415
Step four.

46
00:02:05,175 --> 00:02:06,648
The concentrations level off.

47
00:02:06,648 --> 00:02:08,827
Reactants and products
are both still there.

48
00:02:09,577 --> 00:02:10,891
Here is a surprise.

49
00:02:10,891 --> 00:02:12,579
Start from the other side.

50
00:02:12,929 --> 00:02:17,309
Fill the flask with only hydrogen
iodide, made of the same atoms.

51
00:02:17,709 --> 00:02:22,619
It breaks up into hydrogen and iodine,
until the two speeds are equal again.

52
00:02:23,019 --> 00:02:26,192
At the same temperature, it
ends at the same mixture.

53
00:02:26,192 --> 00:02:28,942
Equilibrium can be reached
from either direction.

54
00:02:29,542 --> 00:02:32,535
But how do we know the
reaction is still running?

55
00:02:32,535 --> 00:02:34,031
Here is a clever test.

56
00:02:34,381 --> 00:02:37,925
Scientists used deuterium,
hydrogen with an extra neutron.

57
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:38,950
A heavy twin.

58
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:42,237
It reacts the same way,
but we can tell it apart.

59
00:02:42,637 --> 00:02:45,429
They made two ammonia
equilibrium mixtures.

60
00:02:45,429 --> 00:02:48,483
One with normal hydrogen,
one with deuterium.

61
00:02:48,883 --> 00:02:50,363
Then they mixed the two.

62
00:02:50,363 --> 00:02:53,355
If the reactions had stopped,
nothing new would form.

63
00:02:53,755 --> 00:02:58,370
But after a while, they found new
molecules: NH two D, NHD two, and

64
00:02:58,370 --> 00:02:59,382
the gas HD.

65
00:02:59,782 --> 00:03:02,431
Hydrogen and deuterium had swapped places.

66
00:03:02,431 --> 00:03:05,392
So the reactions were
still running both ways.

67
00:03:05,392 --> 00:03:06,936
Equilibrium is dynamic.

68
00:03:07,536 --> 00:03:08,436
Compare.

69
00:03:08,175 --> 00:03:10,612
A stopped reaction: no molecule changes.

70
00:03:10,612 --> 00:03:14,579
A reaction at equilibrium: molecules
keep changing, but the amounts do

71
00:03:14,579 --> 00:03:15,479
not.

72
00:03:15,437 --> 00:03:16,558
Pause and think.

73
00:03:16,558 --> 00:03:20,020
At equilibrium, is the rate
of the forward reaction zero?

74
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:24,120
No.

75
00:03:23,647 --> 00:03:24,798
It is not zero.

76
00:03:24,798 --> 00:03:26,952
It is equal to the backward rate.

77
00:03:27,602 --> 00:03:28,737
Remember this.

78
00:03:28,737 --> 00:03:32,205
At equilibrium, forward
rate equals backward rate.

79
00:03:32,205 --> 00:03:33,615
Equal, not zero.

80
00:03:34,315 --> 00:03:36,005
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:36,305 --> 00:03:41,461
One mole per litre each of hydrogen and
iodine are heated in a closed flask.

82
00:03:41,461 --> 00:03:46,387
At equilibrium, zero point two two
moles per litre of hydrogen are left.

83
00:03:46,387 --> 00:03:48,897
Find the iodine and hydrogen iodide.

84
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:52,578
Hydrogen used up: one
minus zero point two two.

85
00:03:52,578 --> 00:03:55,150
Zero point seven eight moles per litre.

86
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,392
One iodine reacts with each hydrogen.

87
00:03:58,392 --> 00:04:01,319
So iodine left is also zero point two two.

88
00:04:02,069 --> 00:04:04,624
Each hydrogen makes two hydrogen iodide.

89
00:04:04,624 --> 00:04:06,837
Two times zero point seven eight.

90
00:04:06,837 --> 00:04:09,206
One point five six moles per litre.

91
00:04:09,956 --> 00:04:11,768
Notice: nothing ran out.

92
00:04:11,768 --> 00:04:14,258
All three are present at equilibrium.

93
00:04:14,908 --> 00:04:16,369
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:16,719 --> 00:04:20,345
Students think: at equilibrium, reactants
and products have equal concentrations.

95
00:04:20,745 --> 00:04:21,645
Wrong.

96
00:04:21,308 --> 00:04:22,681
The rates are equal.

97
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:25,981
The concentrations are
constant, but usually not equal.

98
00:04:26,631 --> 00:04:28,213
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:28,213 --> 00:04:32,290
Boards ask about dynamic
equilibrium and the deuterium proof.

100
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:35,443
Entrance tests ask about
rates at equilibrium.

101
00:04:36,043 --> 00:04:37,260
Exam level now.

102
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:42,096
At equilibrium, the forward reaction
makes HI at zero point zero two zero

103
00:04:42,096 --> 00:04:44,055
moles per litre per second.

104
00:04:44,055 --> 00:04:47,293
How fast does the backward
reaction make H two?

105
00:04:47,293 --> 00:04:48,562
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:53,962 --> 00:04:55,374
The answer is B.

107
00:04:55,374 --> 00:04:59,152
Zero point zero one zero
moles per litre per second.

108
00:04:59,652 --> 00:05:03,426
At equilibrium, HI breaks
down as fast as it forms.

109
00:05:03,426 --> 00:05:05,309
Zero point zero two zero.

110
00:05:05,809 --> 00:05:08,435
Two HI break down to give one H two.

111
00:05:08,435 --> 00:05:10,749
So H two forms at half that rate.

112
00:05:11,249 --> 00:05:15,936
Zero point zero two zero divided
by two is zero point zero one zero.

113
00:05:15,936 --> 00:05:18,400
A thought the reaction had stopped.

114
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,541
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:20,541 --> 00:05:22,125
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:27,511
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:27,911 --> 00:05:28,811
One.

118
00:05:28,394 --> 00:05:30,772
What is equal at chemical equilibrium?

119
00:05:31,572 --> 00:05:32,472
Two.

120
00:05:32,029 --> 00:05:34,873
How did deuterium prove
the reaction never stops?

121
00:05:35,673 --> 00:05:36,573
Three.

122
00:05:36,075 --> 00:05:38,335
Can equilibrium be reached
starting from the products?

123
00:05:39,235 --> 00:05:43,449
Next time: is there one number that
sums up an equilibrium mixture?

124
00:05:43,449 --> 00:05:46,443
We find out in the Law
of Chemical Equilibrium.
